# plans/plan_dx9.md D9-A5 scene corpus: sprite_basic_quad
# The first scene to exercise SpriteBatch (Phase D9-9, D9-90/D9-91/D9-92/D9-93), a genuinely
# different draw model from every prior scene in this corpus: no Effect, no vertex declaration,
# no explicit World/View/Projection -- just SpriteBatch.Begin()/Draw(texture, destRect,
# color)/End() through the real public API, matching D9-93's own explicit requirement to test
# through SpriteBatch/Texture2D, not the raw ISpriteBatchRenderer interface.
#
# This is also the FIRST empirical measurement of D9-91's own half-pixel offset (design
# decision 10): real XNA 4.0's D3D9-era SpriteBatch bakes a half-texel correction into its own
# MatrixTransform to compensate for Direct3D 9's texel-center-at-integer-coordinate convention
# (a convention D3D10+/modern APIs do not share -- see plans/plan_dx9.md's own "Divergence 4"). A 1x1
# solid-color texture stretched into a clean, easily-verified integer destination rectangle
# (x=50,y=60,w=80,h=40 on a 256x256 canvas) sidesteps any point-filter texel-boundary ambiguity
# (the same trick this corpus already established for envmap_quad.scene/dualtexture_quad.scene) --
# if the half-pixel offset is missing, wrong-signed, or off by more than a rounding hair, the
# rendered rectangle's edges will land visibly off from x=[50,130)/y=[60,100), immediately visible
# as a shifted-by-roughly-one-pixel boundary in the pixel diff, not merely a subtle color error.
width=256
height=256
profile=HiDef
clearcolor=100,149,237,255
spritebatchmode=true
texturewidth=1
textureheight=1
# texturefilter is intentionally NOT set here -- SpriteBatch.Begin() with no arguments uses real
# XNA's own default (SamplerState.LinearClamp), and with a 1x1 texture linear vs. point filtering
# is indistinguishable anyway (a single texel has nothing to interpolate against).
texturepixel=255,0,0,255
spritedestrect=50,60,80,40
spritecolor=255,255,255,255
